  "problem": "$\\,$ If you don't buy a ticket, then you cannot enter the concert.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?",
  "worked_solution": "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You don't buy a ticket.\n\n- $B:$ You cannot enter the concert.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A\u21d2B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n\u00acA\u2228B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $\u00acA:$ You buy a ticket.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You buy a ticket, or you cannot enter the concert.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Buy a ticket, or you cannot enter the concert.",
